  "Born Robert Williams Buchanan at Caverswall, Staffordshire, on 18 August 1841; he made his name with Undertones (1863) and Idyls and Legends of Inverburn (1865), attacked Rossetti and the Pre-Raphaelites in 'The Fleshly School of Poetry' (1871), and wrote the novels The Shadow of the Sword (1876) and God and the Man (1881). He died in Streatham on 10 June 1901.",
  "Robert Buchanan was the most combative man of letters of the Victorian age: his attack on the Pre-Raphaelites in 'The Fleshly School of Poetry' is the most famous act of literary criticism of the century, and his social poems and novels made him the leading literary socialist of the 1880s.",
